---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import DemoReport from '../components/DemoReport.astro';
import '../styles/how.css';
---

<Layout
  lang="en"
  title="How it works — loam.dev"
  description="How loam.dev analyses a Dart/Flutter project: resolve it like the compiler, run the rules across every library, fingerprint each Finding, compare against your baseline — and fail CI only on what is new."
  path="/how-it-works"
>
  <span id="status" class="sr-only" role="status" aria-live="polite"></span>

  <header class="scene page-head how-head" aria-labelledby="how-title" data-testid="how-head">
    <div class="wrap page-head__grid intro">
      <p class="label">How it works</p>
      <h1 id="how-title" class="display">How it works<span class="accent">.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead page-head__lead">loam.dev loads your whole Dart/Flutter project the way the compiler sees it, runs
      its rules across every library at once, and turns the result into a clear pass or
      fail for CI. A baseline accepts the code you already have, so only problems added
      after that can block a merge. Six stages, no guessing, no text matching.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- Pipeline: sticky stage index (desktop) beside the six stages. -->
  <section class="scene how-pipe" aria-labelledby="pipe-title" data-testid="how-pipeline">
    <div class="wrap how-pipe__grid">
      <div class="how-pipe__aside">
        <p class="label">Six stages</p>
        <h2 id="pipe-title" class="h2">The pipeline</h2>
        <nav class="how-pipe__index" aria-label="Pipeline stages">
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#stage-loader" data-testid="how-stage-link-loader"><span>01</span>Loader</a></li>
            <li><a href="#stage-rules" data-testid="how-stage-link-rules"><span>02</span>Rules</a></li>
            <li><a href="#stage-findings" data-testid="how-stage-link-findings"><span>03</span>Findings</a></li>
            <li><a href="#stage-baseline" data-testid="how-stage-link-baseline"><span>04</span>Baseline</a></li>
            <li><a href="#stage-gate" data-testid="how-stage-link-gate"><span>05</span>Gate</a></li>
            <li><a href="#stage-report" data-testid="how-stage-link-report"><span>06</span>Report</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>
      </div>
      <ol class="stages">
          <li class="stage rv" id="stage-loader" aria-labelledby="stage-loader-title">
            <span class="stage__num" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
            <h3 id="stage-loader-title" class="h3 stage__name">Loader</h3>
            <p class="stage__text">The <code>ProjectLoader</code> hands your entire project to Dart’s own <code>AnalysisContextCollection</code>. Every file is parsed and resolved into
              a typed element model — the same one the Dart SDK works with. So the rules
              know which declaration a name points to, not just which text matches.</p>
          </li>

          <li class="stage rv" id="stage-rules" aria-labelledby="stage-rules-title">
            <span class="stage__num" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
            <h3 id="stage-rules-title" class="h3 stage__name">Rules</h3>
            <p class="stage__text">Each <strong>Rule</strong> is a self-contained unit behind one shared
              interface and runs over the resolved project. A new capability is a new rule;
              the pipeline itself never changes. All live rules are deterministic: offline,
              free and reproducible. A planned, optional LLM-assisted rule will only reach
              CI through a verdict cache.</p>
          </li>

          <li class="stage rv" id="stage-findings" aria-labelledby="stage-findings-title">
            <span class="stage__num" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
            <h3 id="stage-findings-title" class="h3 stage__name">Findings</h3>
            <p class="stage__text">Every rule reports <strong>Findings</strong> of the form <code>&#123; ruleId, severity, location, message, fingerprint &#125;</code>.
              The <strong>Fingerprint</strong> is a stable hash that survives reformatting
              and shifted line numbers, so the same problem stays the same Finding. Each
              Finding also names what kind of problem it is and what to do about it —
              written so an AI agent can act on it without guessing.</p>
          </li>

          <li class="stage rv" id="stage-baseline" aria-labelledby="stage-baseline-title">
            <span class="stage__num" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
            <h3 id="stage-baseline-title" class="h3 stage__name">Baseline</h3>
            <p class="stage__text">The <strong>Baseline</strong> holds the Findings you accept, in <code>baseline.json</code>. To bring an existing project in, run a full audit,
              fix what matters and freeze the rest with <code>loam baseline --write</code>.
              From then on the Gate only cares about <em>new</em> Findings — not the ones
              you already know.</p>
          </li>

          <li class="stage rv" id="stage-gate" aria-labelledby="stage-gate-title">
            <span class="stage__num" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
            <h3 id="stage-gate-title" class="h3 stage__name">Gate</h3>
            <p class="stage__text">The <strong>Gate</strong> is the decision for CI: it compares the current
              Findings with the Baseline and answers with an exit code. Nothing new: exit 0.
              A new Finding: exit 1. Rule profiles decide what may block — <code>core</code> rules by default, <code>strict</code> ones when you opt in.</p>
          </li>

          <li class="stage rv" id="stage-report" aria-labelledby="stage-report-title">
            <span class="stage__num" aria-hidden="true">06</span>
            <h3 id="stage-report-title" class="h3 stage__name">Report</h3>
            <p class="stage__text">The <strong>Reporter</strong> writes for whoever reads it: <code>human</code> in the terminal, <code>json</code> / <code>sarif</code> / <code>markdown</code> for CI and tools, <code>agent</code> for a compact, capped list of Findings, and <code>html</code> for a self-contained report in which
              you select Findings and copy a ready-made fix-prompt. After a run with
              Findings, loam.dev adds one <strong>preventive recommendation</strong> per rule
              class that fired — deterministic, no LLM call — for your agent to propose for
              its standing instructions.</p>
          </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <DemoReport lang="en" />

  <!-- loam scan vs loam gate -->
  <section class="scene how-compare" aria-labelledby="compare-title" data-testid="how-compare">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="how-compare__head">
        <p class="label">Two commands</p>
        <h2 id="compare-title" class="h2">loam scan vs. loam gate</h2>
        <p class="lead">Two commands, two jobs. Pick the one that fits the moment.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="compare">
        <article class="compare__item compare__item--scan rv" aria-labelledby="compare-scan">
          <p class="label compare__tag">Full audit · ignores the baseline</p>
          <h3 id="compare-scan" class="h3"><code>loam scan</code></h3>
          <p class="body-copy">Runs every active Rule over the whole project and shows <em>all</em> Findings,
            whatever the Baseline says. Use it when you bring loam.dev into an existing
            repo: see the full picture, fix what matters, then freeze the rest with <code>loam baseline --write</code>.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="compare__item compare__item--gate rv" aria-labelledby="compare-gate">
          <p class="label compare__tag">CI gate · ratchet by default</p>
          <h3 id="compare-gate" class="h3"><code>loam gate</code></h3>
          <p class="body-copy">The everyday command for CI. In <strong>ratchet mode</strong>, the default, only
            Findings that are <em>not</em> in the Baseline fail the build — the code can only
            get better over time. For new projects or pipelines that allow no Findings at
            all, <code>--absolute</code> ignores the Baseline and applies a fixed threshold
            instead.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="body-copy how-compare__flow">A typical start: <code>loam scan</code> → fix what matters → <code>loam baseline --write</code> → <code>loam gate</code> in CI from then on.
        While you code, <code>loam watch</code> repeats the full audit after every saved
        change and shows what is new or resolved.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Semantics over syntax -->
  <section class="scene how-sem" aria-labelledby="sem-title" data-testid="how-semantics">
    <div class="wrap how-sem__grid">
      <div class="how-sem__head">
        <p class="label">Design decision</p>
        <h2 id="sem-title" class="h2">Semantics over syntax<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">A central design decision in loam.dev is what the analysis runs <em>on</em>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="how-sem__body body-copy">
  <p>
    <code>dart analyze</code> is good at what it checks: type errors, missing
    imports, lint rules — mostly one declaration or file at a time. It doesn’t follow the <em>graph</em> of references across your whole project: which public members are
    used anywhere at all, which libraries import each other in a circle, which helper
    exists twice under different names.
  </p>

  <p>
    Text matching can’t answer those questions reliably. A regex can’t tell a
    declaration from a reference, a local variable from a public export, or generated
    code from code you wrote.
  </p>

  <p>
    loam.dev works on the <strong>resolved element model</strong>: every symbol is a
    typed element with one identity, and every reference points to exactly that element.
    The rules work on that model, not on raw text. In practice:
  </p>

  <ul class="how-sem__list">
    <li>A name that merely looks like another one is never mistaken for it.</li>
    <li>Generated files and code-generator inputs are recognised automatically.</li>
    <li>Renaming a class changes nothing — what counts is whether it is referenced, not
    what it is called.</li>
    <li>References across libraries and packages are followed correctly.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    That is what “semantics over syntax” means: the rules see your code the way the
    compiler does, not the way a text editor does.
  </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Next step -->
  <section class="scene how-try" aria-labelledby="try-title" data-testid="how-try">
    <div class="wrap how-try__grid">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Get started</p>
        <h2 id="try-title" class="h2">Try it on your project</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="how-try__cmds">
        <div class="cmd">
          <span class="cmd__p" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
          <code class="cmd__t" id="how-cmd-pub">dart pub global activate loam</code>
          <button class="cmd__copy" type="button" data-copy-target="how-cmd-pub" data-track="how" data-testid="how-copy-pub" data-done="copied" data-failed="copy failed" aria-label="Copy install command">copy</button>
        </div>
        <div class="cmd cmd--secondary">
          <span class="cmd__p" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
          <code class="cmd__t"><span id="how-cmd-scan">loam scan</span><span class="muted">  # in your project</span></code>
          <button class="cmd__copy" type="button" data-copy-target="how-cmd-scan" data-track="how" data-testid="how-copy-scan" data-done="copied" data-failed="copy failed" aria-label="Copy scan command">copy</button>
        </div>
        <p class="body-copy how-try__text">The <a class="link" href="/developer-guide/" data-testid="how-link-guide">Developer Guide</a> covers the full CLI reference,
        output formats, configuration and worked examples — including the complete
        onboarding sequence and how to run <code>loam gate</code> in CI. Every rule, with
        examples, is on the <a class="link" href="/rules/" data-testid="how-link-rules">rules page</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

